@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Setting up the game area") {
        In this first part, we're going to set up the game area. Let's get started by
        importing the start assets and setting up the game scene.

        We've prepared a Godot project with the 3D models and sounds we'll use for this
        tutorial, linked in the index page. If you haven't done so yet, you can download
        the archive here:
        [Squash the Creeps assets](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-docs-project-starters/releases/download/latest-4.x/3d%5Fsquash%5Fthe%5Fcreeps%5Fstarter.zip).
    }
    @Section(title: "Importing the start assets") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Once you downloaded it, extract the .zip archive on your computer. Open the
            Godot Project Manager and click the *Import* button.

            @Image(source: 01.import_button.webp)

            In the import popup, enter the full path to the freshly created directory
            `3d_squash_the_creeps_starter/`. You can click the *Browse* button on the
            right to open a file browser and navigate to the `project.godot` file the
            folder contains.

            @Image(source: 02.browse_to_project_folder.webp)

            Click *Import* to open the project in the editor.

            @Image(source: 03.import_and_edit.webp)

            A window notifying you that the project was generated by an older Godot
            version may appear. Click *OK* to convert the project to your current Godot
            version.

            @Image(source: import_project_to_4.x_prompt.webp)

            If it doesn't open immediately open the project from your project list.

            The start project contains an icon and two folders: `art/` and `fonts/`.
            There, you will find the art assets and music we'll use in the game.

            @Image(source: 04.start_assets.webp)

            There are two 3D models, `player.glb` and `mob.glb`, some materials that
            belong to these models, and a music track.
        }
        @Steps {
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Setting up the playable area") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We're going to create our main scene with a plain
            [Node](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fnode.html#class-node)
            as its root. In the *Scene* dock, click the *Add Child Node* button
            represented by a "+" icon in the top-left and double-click on *Node*. Name
            the node `Main`. An alternate method to rename the node is to right-click
            on *Node* and choose *Rename* (or F2). Alternatively, to add a node to the
            scene, you can press Ctrl + A (Cmd + A on macOS).

            @Image(source: 05.main_node.webp)

            Save the scene as `main.tscn` by pressing Ctrl + S (Cmd + S on macOS).

            We'll start by adding a floor that'll prevent the characters from falling.
            To create static colliders like the floor, walls, or ceilings, you can use
            [StaticBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fstaticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d)
            nodes. They require
            [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
            child nodes to define the collision area. With the `Main` node selected,
            add a
            [StaticBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fstaticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d)
            node, then a
            [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d).
            Rename the
            [StaticBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fstaticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d)
            to `Ground`.

            @Image(source: adding_static_body3D.webp)

            Your scene tree should look like this

            @Image(source: 06.staticbody_node.webp)

            A warning sign next to the
            [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
            appears because we haven't defined its shape. If you click the icon, a
            popup appears to give you more information.

            @Image(source: 07.collision_shape_warning.webp)

            To create a shape, select the
            [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
            node, head to the *Inspector* and click the *<empty>* field next to the
            *Shape* property. Create a new *BoxShape3D*.

            @Image(source: 08.create_box_shape3D.webp)

            The box shape is perfect for flat ground and walls. Its thickness makes it
            reliable to block even fast-moving objects.

            A box's wireframe appears in the viewport with three orange dots. You can
            click and drag these to edit the shape's extents interactively. We can
            also precisely set the size in the inspector. Click on the
            [BoxShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fboxshape3d.html#class-boxshape3d)
            to expand the resource. Set its *Size* to `60` on the X-axis, `2` for the
            Y-axis, and `60` for the Z-axis.

            @Image(source: 09.box_size.webp)

            Collision shapes are invisible. We need to add a visual floor that goes
            along with it. Select the `Ground` node and add a
            [MeshInstance3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fmeshinstance3d.html#class-meshinstance3d)
            as its child.

            @Image(source: 10.mesh_instance3d.webp)

            In the *Inspector*, click on the field next to *Mesh* and create a
            [BoxMesh](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fboxmesh.html#class-boxmesh)
            resource to create a visible box.

            @Image(source: 11.box_mesh.webp)

            Once again, it's too small by default. Click the box icon to expand the
            resource and set its *Size* to `60`, `2`, and `60`.

            @Image(source: 12.cube_resized.webp)

            You should see a wide grey slab that covers the grid and blue and red axes
            in the viewport.

            We're going to move the ground down so we can see the floor grid. To do
            this, the grid snapping feature can be used. Grid snapping can be activated
            2 ways in the 3D editor. The first is by pressing the *Use Snap* button
            (or pressing the Y key). The second is by selecting a node, dragging a
            handle on the gizmo **then** holding Ctrl (Cmd on macOS) while holding the
            selected node.

            @Image(source: use_snap.webp)

            Start by setting snapping with your preferred method. Then move the
            `Ground` node using the Y-axis (the green arrow on the gizmo).

            @Image(source: move_gizmo_y_axis.webp)

            > Note: If you can't see the 3D object manipulator like on the image above,
            > ensure the *Transform Mode* is active in the toolbar above the view.

            @Image(source: 14.select_mode_icon.webp)

            Move the ground down `1` meter, in order to have a visible editor grid. A
            label in the bottom-left corner of the viewport tells you how much you're
            translating the node.

            @Image(source: 15.translation_amount.png)

            > Note: Moving the *Ground* node down moves both children along with it.
            > Ensure you move the *Ground* node, **not** the *MeshInstance3D* or the
            > *CollisionShape3D*.

            Ultimately, `Ground`'s transform.position.y should be -1

            @Image(source: ground_down1meter.webp)

            Let's add a directional light so our scene isn't all grey. Select the
            `Main` node and add a child node
            [DirectionalLight3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fdirectionallight3d.html#class-directionallight3d).

            @Image(source: create_directional_light3d.webp)

            We need to move and rotate the
            [DirectionalLight3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fdirectionallight3d.html#class-directionallight3d)
            node. Move it up by clicking and dragging on the manipulator's green arrow
            and click and drag on the red arc to rotate it around the X-axis, until
            the ground is lit.

            In the *Inspector*, turn on *Shadow* by clicking the checkbox.

            @Image(source: 16.turn_on_shadows.webp)

            At this point, your project should look like this.

            @Image(source: 17.project_with_light.webp)

            That's our starting point. In the next part, we will work on the player
            scene and base movement.
        }
        @Steps {
        }
    }
}
